1
00:00:00,142 --> 00:00:08,320
What if I told you that a single Stanford research paper might just render NVIDIA most expensive memory chip completely unnecessary?

2
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:11,204
Here's the thing about the AI industry right now.

3
00:00:11,630 --> 00:00:13,284
We have hit a massive wall.

4
00:00:13,614 --> 00:00:20,388
Everyone talks about compute limits, but the real silent killer of traditional language models is memory bandwidth.

5
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:23,600
We physically cannot build memory chips fast enough.

6
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,400
We need them to feed these massive AI models.

7
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:29,440
Let us back up and look at how things work today.

8
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:34,148
You can think of a standard language model as the gigantic matrix of numbers.

9
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:39,076
You give this model a prompt, it predicts the next most likely token.

10
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:42,020
A token is just a short piece of word.

11
00:00:42,286 --> 00:00:46,148
It predicts your token based on your prompt and its training data.

12
00:00:46,414 --> 00:00:48,100
Then it repeats this process.

13
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:51,396
It moves forward one token at a time.

14
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,040
It does this until the entire answer is complete.

15
00:00:55,214 --> 00:00:58,148
It is basically a really big autocomplete system.

16
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:01,124
This process takes a huge amount of resources.

17
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:05,700
To get a 1000-word answer, the model runs 1000 times.

18
00:01:06,158 --> 00:01:07,780
It runs in a strict sequence.

19
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,484
During every single pass, the graphic card needs to read the model parameters.

20
00:01:12,814 --> 00:01:14,532
It reads them from the video memory.

21
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,836
A massive model has trillions of numbers.

22
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:21,440
The processor has to load these numbers 1000 separate times.

23
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:23,620
This method is incredibly slow.

24
00:01:24,046 --> 00:01:26,916
It also consumes a massive amount of electricity.

25
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:31,140
But most importantly, it requires extreme memory bandwidth.

26
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,628
The speed of the memory limits the speed of the AI.

27
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:37,092
The processor often sits there.

28
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:43,280
It waits for the memory to send the data, and the problem gets even worse with long conversations.

29
00:01:43,534 --> 00:01:46,468
The model has to remember all the previous tokens.

30
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,960
It stores them in a temporary cache.

31
00:01:49,166 --> 00:01:51,204
We will call this a KV cache.

32
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,756
As the conversations get longer, this cache grows.

33
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,360
In long context models, this memory cache grows way too fast.

34
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,920
It eats up all the available video memory.

35
00:02:02,126 --> 00:02:06,340
Hardware companies try to fix this by packing more memory onto the cards.

36
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:11,364
They use high bandwidth memory, but these chips are incredibly expensive.

37
00:02:11,950 --> 00:02:14,148
They are also very hard to manufacture.

38
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:17,124
This creates a massive hardware bottleneck.

39
00:02:17,678 --> 00:02:19,200
So how do we fix this?

40
00:02:19,374 --> 00:02:21,988
Without just buying more expensive memory chips.

41
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:26,468
A research team from Stanford University recently dropped a new architecture.

42
00:02:26,862 --> 00:02:30,628
It is called next concept modeling, or NCP for short.

43
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:33,200
NCP takes a radically different approach.

44
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:35,600
It generates the entire answer in parallel.

45
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:39,680
To do this, the researchers borrowed an idea from image generation models.

46
00:02:39,854 --> 00:02:42,212
Think about how an AI generates a picture.

47
00:02:42,606 --> 00:02:44,480
It starts with the blurry layer.

48
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:46,756
This layer represents a rough image.

49
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:49,476
Then the next layer adds more details.

50
00:02:50,030 --> 00:02:52,720
You repeat this until you get a sharp final image.

51
00:02:52,910 --> 00:02:55,972
NCP does this exact same thing with text.

52
00:02:56,558 --> 00:02:58,800
Words live inside a collection of layers.

53
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:02,308
At the top layer you have a very rough outline of a concept.

54
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,932
The researchers call these concepts discrete codes.

55
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:08,320
The code is just a tiny digital representation of an idea.

56
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:11,428
In each new layer, the concept gets refined.

57
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,780
The model stores only the new requirements as additional codes.

58
00:03:16,334 --> 00:03:20,740
So every subsequent layer has more codes than the layer before it.

59
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:23,204
This continues down to the bottom layer.

60
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:27,556
At this final layer, every code becomes a real output token.

61
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,880
If you combine all these layers, you get the final text.

62
00:03:31,054 --> 00:03:33,444
So you ask the NCM model a question.

63
00:03:33,870 --> 00:03:36,480
The model runs exactly one time per layer.

64
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,780
Each layer predicts the entire output in parallel.

65
00:03:40,174 --> 00:03:42,084
The top layers are very coarse.

66
00:03:42,638 --> 00:03:49,828
If you look at the words in the top layer, they would look like complete garbage, but the fidelity increases layer by layer.

67
00:03:50,030 --> 00:03:53,732
By the final pass, you get a high-quality text output.

68
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:56,228
This text is the answer to your prompt.

69
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:59,172
Let us go back to our 1000 word example.

70
00:03:59,598 --> 00:04:03,332
A traditional model takes 1000 passes to write that answer.

71
00:04:03,726 --> 00:04:07,520
The Stanford NCM model takes just 32 passes.

72
00:04:07,694 --> 00:04:12,400
The graphics card only reads 32 gigabytes of memory instead of 1000.

73
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:15,172
This is a massive drop in memory bandwidth.

74
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:21,444
It is more than 30 times as efficient, but generating everything in parallel creates a huge problem.

75
00:04:21,998 --> 00:04:28,772
How do you force an AI to actually learn a high level concept instead of just memorizing the training data?

76
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:32,560
Wait, how does a computer even know what a concept is?

77
00:04:32,846 --> 00:04:35,200
This brings us to the second technical secret.

78
00:04:35,374 --> 00:04:38,404
The Stanford researchers used a brutal training trick.

79
00:04:39,086 --> 00:04:40,932
They called it a nested dropout.

80
00:04:41,216 --> 00:04:45,136
They use a special program to turn text into those discrete codes.

81
00:04:45,296 --> 00:04:49,604
During training, the researchers randomly drop the detailed bottom layers.

82
00:04:49,776 --> 00:04:55,940
Imagine you need to study for a big math test, but someone rips out the last three chapters of your textbook.

83
00:04:56,398 --> 00:04:58,852
You have to deeply understand the core concepts.

84
00:04:59,246 --> 00:05:02,336
You cannot just memorize the formulas at the end of the book.

85
00:05:02,542 --> 00:05:05,956
If you are enjoying our chat, I have something important to share with you.

86
00:05:06,990 --> 00:05:10,308
Looks like YouTube algorithm is not a big fan of my videos.

87
00:05:11,086 --> 00:05:13,136
This is why, guys, I need your support.

88
00:05:13,422 --> 00:05:16,656
Hit the like button to tell the Google that the content is dope.

89
00:05:16,816 --> 00:05:21,216
If you use social media and have friends in tech, please share this video.

90
00:05:21,454 --> 00:05:24,896
If you are not subscribed yet, please do get subscribed right now.

91
00:05:25,216 --> 00:05:29,380
YouTube algorithm should know that the tech community is not that.

92
00:05:29,616 --> 00:05:35,268
But getting back to our topic, nested dropouts does exactly this to the AI model.

93
00:05:35,456 --> 00:05:37,444
It puts a lot of pressure on the system.

94
00:05:37,696 --> 00:05:42,756
It forces the top layers to pack maximum meaning into a tiny sequence of codes.

95
00:05:43,056 --> 00:05:47,076
The model cannot rely on the bottom layers to fix the output layer.

96
00:05:47,376 --> 00:05:53,444
The bottom layers might be missing entirely, so the top layers actually learn true concepts.

97
00:05:53,870 --> 00:05:56,496
They do not just output compressed static.

98
00:05:56,656 --> 00:06:00,068
This is why NCM actually understands what it writes.

99
00:06:00,336 --> 00:06:04,100
It learns the core idea before it writes the specific words.

100
00:06:04,416 --> 00:06:05,828
That is actually wild.

101
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:09,988
But this course to find design has a major vulnerability.

102
00:06:10,176 --> 00:06:14,016
What happens if the model makes a mistake at the very top layer?

103
00:06:14,222 --> 00:06:16,260
This brings us to compounding errors.

104
00:06:16,782 --> 00:06:22,116
If the top layer hallucinates a bad concept, that mistake ripples down.

105
00:06:22,510 --> 00:06:26,692
It corrupts the final text, it creates a total error death spiral.

106
00:06:26,976 --> 00:06:31,044
To fix this, the team engineered something called conditioning corruption.

107
00:06:31,438 --> 00:06:33,220
This is our second technical secret.

108
00:06:33,456 --> 00:06:37,508
During training, they deliberately inject wrong codes into the middle layers.

109
00:06:37,936 --> 00:06:42,404
They use corrupted data, but they keep the correct final answers intact.

110
00:06:42,576 --> 00:06:45,220
Imagine you teach a kid to ride a bike.

111
00:06:45,696 --> 00:06:48,804
You randomly push the bike slightly off balance.

112
00:06:49,376 --> 00:06:56,336
The kid has to learn how to correct their posture, they learn to recover their balance before they crash into a tree.

113
00:06:56,526 --> 00:06:59,076
Conditioning corruption does this for the AI.

114
00:06:59,296 --> 00:07:01,828
It trains the lower levels to be resilient.

115
00:07:02,096 --> 00:07:05,540
It forces the model to self-correct its own mistakes.

116
00:07:06,158 --> 00:07:09,572
It fixes bad concepts before they turn into garbage tags.

117
00:07:09,936 --> 00:07:15,296
Because nobody wants their AI to confidently output complete garbage faster than before.

118
00:07:15,536 --> 00:07:18,180
But now we have a model that is fast.

119
00:07:18,574 --> 00:07:22,020
It is memory efficient and it fixes its own errors.

120
00:07:22,416 --> 00:07:24,548
But it is still a very small model.

121
00:07:24,942 --> 00:07:30,276
How does a tiny model compete with the reasoning power of a massive frontier model?

122
00:07:30,496 --> 00:07:34,148
This is where we see a massive developer trend for 2026.

123
00:07:34,496 --> 00:07:36,228
It is called test time scaling.

124
00:07:36,656 --> 00:07:39,012
And this is our third technical secret.

125
00:07:39,534 --> 00:07:41,444
NCM is extremely fast.

126
00:07:41,616 --> 00:07:45,764
It generates entire blocks of text in a fraction of the normal time.

127
00:07:45,936 --> 00:07:48,868
You can use this save time to buy more intelligence.

128
00:07:49,326 --> 00:07:54,180
Instead of just taking the first answer, you generate 20 different candidate answers.

129
00:07:54,638 --> 00:07:56,708
You generate all of them in parallel.

130
00:07:57,006 --> 00:07:58,976
Then you run a mathematical filter.

131
00:07:59,246 --> 00:08:03,108
The filter is called minimum Bayes risk or MBR.

132
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:10,468
MBR looks at all 20 answers, it mathematically selects the candidate that has the highest consensus among the groups.

133
00:08:10,702 --> 00:08:13,188
Imagine you have a really tough coding problem.

134
00:08:13,486 --> 00:08:18,788
You could ask one senior developer for an answer, or you could ask 20 junior developers.

135
00:08:18,976 --> 00:08:21,796
Then you find the common threats in all the answers.

136
00:08:22,176 --> 00:08:25,124
You mathematically pick the best overall solution.

137
00:08:25,456 --> 00:08:30,212
You literally trade the model's extreme speed to boost its reasoning at runtime.

138
00:08:30,496 --> 00:08:35,396
You do not need to train a bigger model, you just run the small model multiple times.

139
00:08:35,982 --> 00:08:39,616
The researchers tested this exact theory on games of Sudoku.

140
00:08:40,016 --> 00:08:43,376
They used a 25 million parameter NCM model.

141
00:08:43,790 --> 00:08:47,428
It solves 86 out of 100 hardboards.

142
00:08:47,872 --> 00:08:52,900
The researchers compared these results against the standard language model of the same size.

143
00:08:53,390 --> 00:08:55,940
The standard model solved exactly one board.

144
00:08:56,272 --> 00:08:59,140
That is an 8600% improvement.

145
00:08:59,598 --> 00:09:00,740
I love this part.

146
00:09:01,166 --> 00:09:04,832
It shows how speed can directly translate into better logic.

147
00:09:04,992 --> 00:09:09,472
But why should the massive hardware companies care about an AI playing Sudoku?

148
00:09:09,872 --> 00:09:13,632
Because this paper exposes a fatal flaw in the current AI economy.

149
00:09:13,872 --> 00:09:17,092
NCM shifts the main bottleneck entirely.

150
00:09:17,486 --> 00:09:21,508
It moves the bottleneck from the memory bandwidth to pure compute.

151
00:09:21,742 --> 00:09:24,132
This is the billion dollar hardware flip.

152
00:09:24,352 --> 00:09:26,432
As always, there is no free launch.

153
00:09:26,592 --> 00:09:29,124
Look at the bottom layers of the NCM model.

154
00:09:29,550 --> 00:09:33,028
It has four times as many codes as output tokens.

155
00:09:33,312 --> 00:09:36,932
Evaluating all those extra codes takes more processing power.

156
00:09:37,422 --> 00:09:43,792
The architecture drastically reduces the memory bandwidth you need, but it requires more raw compute.

157
00:09:44,032 --> 00:09:48,932
Right now, the industry buys expensive NVIDIA chips, mostly for their memory.

158
00:09:49,262 --> 00:09:52,516
We need that high bandwidth memory to hold the massive cache.

159
00:09:52,992 --> 00:09:56,612
But NCM does not need that massive memory bandwidth.

160
00:09:56,752 --> 00:09:58,116
It just needs raw compute.

161
00:09:58,512 --> 00:10:03,780
This means we could theoretically run frontier level AI on a consumer-grade silicon.

162
00:10:04,206 --> 00:10:09,028
You could use standard chips that have massive compute but limited video memory.

163
00:10:09,312 --> 00:10:12,672
This makes the current token by token paradigm look bloated.

164
00:10:12,992 --> 00:10:14,512
It makes it look like a relic.

165
00:10:14,672 --> 00:10:20,292
An algorithmic left field breakthrough might actually solve our massive hardware supply chain problem.

166
00:10:20,512 --> 00:10:23,876
It threatens to upend the entire hardware monopoly.

167
00:10:24,366 --> 00:10:28,100
Will NCM hold up when we scale it to trillions of parameters?

168
00:10:28,512 --> 00:10:30,084
We do not really know yet.

169
00:10:30,510 --> 00:10:32,192
But it proves something important.

170
00:10:32,526 --> 00:10:36,420
We should not accept today's hardware's limits as permanent laws of physics.

171
00:10:36,752 --> 00:10:41,552
New model architectures show us that a massive breakthrough are still possible.

172
00:10:41,806 --> 00:10:46,564
To sum it all up, NCM ditches token by token generation entirely.

173
00:10:46,958 --> 00:10:49,316
It generates text in parallel layers.

174
00:10:49,712 --> 00:10:54,340
It uses nested dropouts to force the model to learn real concepts.

175
00:10:54,592 --> 00:10:57,952
It uses condition and corruption to fix its own errors.

176
00:10:58,062 --> 00:11:02,500
And it uses test time scaling to artificially boost its reasoning.

177
00:11:03,054 --> 00:11:05,764
It trades memory bandwidth for pure compute.

178
00:11:05,952 --> 00:11:17,540
If you want to stay ahead of these massive algorithmic shifts, hit the like button, subscribe to the channel, and let me know in the comments below if you think consumer graphic cards will finally run frontier models.

179
00:11:17,712 --> 00:11:18,752
Thank you for watching.

180
00:11:19,022 --> 00:11:20,260
See you in the next one.

181
00:11:20,752 --> 00:11:21,392
Bye.

